== Why fare integration?

Right now, taking a trip between the same two points using different operators can result in very different costs. A common way for South Side residents to move north-south is to take a bus to the Dan Ryan "L", where they only have to pay the low bus fare of \$2.25 once and get free transfers; very few residents take a bus to the Metra Electric, even if it would be faster, since they'd have to pay both the bus fare of \$2.25 and the Metra fare of \$3.75, plus another CTA or Metra fare if they connected to another line downtown. 

Suburbanites don't often use Pace buses to reach Metra stations, usually preferring to drive or be dropped off, since it's simply more expensive to use the buses. Additionally, it's quite rare for riders to connect between different Pace buses using Metra, or to connect to CTA services in the Loop to access the rest of the city.

If the fares for a travel between two points were the same across the different operators and modes, people would use the mode that is the fastest and most convenient, making the system more efficient and improving the daily lives of the people. Additionally, people who currently see it as inconvenient to take multiple different operators -- for example, commuting from the western suburbs to Evanston by taking a Pace bus to a Metra train and switching to the "L" in the city -- might choose to use transit for their trips, increasing ridership. This will also allow future changes to make the buses and trains be timetabled and planned together, with timed transfers between the modes. Such a system would allow many people for which transit is currently inconvenient to use it, and thus increase ridership.

== What does the legislature want from the fare system?

In SB 2111, ILGA required:
- No fare increases between summer 2026 and summer 2027 (#link("https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/104/PDF/104-0457.pdf#page=144")[70 ILCS 3615/2.04(a)])
- A "regionally coordinated and consolidated fare collection system" (#link("https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/104/PDF/104-0457.pdf#page=147")[70 ILCS 3615/2.04(d)])
- A "next generation fare collection system" (#link("https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/104/PDF/104-0457.pdf#page=148")[70 ILCS 3615/2.04(h)])
- Before summer 2028: (#link("https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/104/PDF/104-0457.pdf#page=150")[70 ILCS 3615/2.04(j)(4)])
  - Income-based reduced fare programs for:
    - veterans
    - formerly incarcareated adults
    - homeless people
  - Reduced fare program for domestic violence and sexual assault survivors
  - Fare capping

Additionally, ILGA required in SB 2111 (#link("https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/104/PDF/104-0457.pdf#page=289")[70 ILCS 3615/3B.27]) that NICTD be permitted to pick up passengers at stations shared with the Metra Electric District. In order to gain the most value from doing so, NICTD fares should be integrated with Metra fares. Additionally, we propose other improvements to NICTD and Metra Electric scheduling that depend on fare integration between the two systems in #cross-link("/phase-1/me-ssl.typ")[Phase 1's ME/SSL plan].

== What else do we want from the fare system?

=== Lower operating costs for the regional rail system

In SB 2111 (#link("https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/104/PDF/104-0457.pdf#page=306")[70 ILCS 3615/4.01(j)]), ILGA required that NITA realize significant savings for the 2026-2027 fiscal year. Additionally, in order to deliver our proposed major service increases to the regional rail system at a reasonable operating cost, we must drastically reduce cost per revenue hour. We show later that better fare policy can drastically reduce our operating costs.

